{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Pi-ha-Hirot.</b> It is what is called Pi-ne-ha-Hirot (Numbers 33:8), and it is so called because pi ('mouth of') is in pne ('face of'), and it is a place-name." "<b>Before Baal-zephon.</b> They said that the magicians of Egypt made, by means of the constellations, figures of copper, and this is Baal-zephon, so that no slave could flee from Egypt past the figure. And because of this it is written that the people fled. But in my opinion, since Moses said to Pharaoh, 'a journey of three days we shall go into the wilderness' (Exodus 8:23), it appeared to Pharaoh from his words that Moses knew the road by which they would go to the place where they would sacrifice there. And when Pharaoh heard that Israel began to go by way of the wilderness, and after they had gone far they turned back to go by another road, then Pharaoh thought that everything Moses had spoken was with cunning, that his intention was not to sacrifice but to flee, for the one who flees loses his way and does not know which road to take." "<b>Opposite it.</b> This word [נִכְחוֹ] comes with a chirik, like אַחֲרֵי נִפְלוֹ (2 Samuel 1:10), and many like this."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:34:20.907855101Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}